.\" mkfs.filsys.1 - part of filsys 1.0.0
.TH MKFS.FILSYS 1 "August 2026" "filsys 1.0.0" "File Format and Disk Utilities"
.SH NAME
mkfs.filsys \- create a V7 filesystem in a disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkfs.filsys
.RB [ \-o
.IR block ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR boot ]
.I image
.RI [ blocks ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkfs.filsys
builds a fresh Seventh Edition Unix filesystem inside a disk image file: a
superblock, a zeroed i-list, an interleaved free-block list, and an empty root
directory.  The image is opened read-write, created if it does not exist, and
grown to the filesystem size.
.PP
A Research Unix disk image holds one or more filesystems
.RI ( partitions ),
selected by a partition table that is compiled into the kernel, not stored on
the disk.  By default the filesystem is written at block 0 (the first
partition); the
.B \-o
option places it at a different block offset within the image.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " block"
Place the filesystem at
.I block
(512-byte blocks) within the image, instead of block 0.
.TP
.BI \-b " boot"
Write
.I boot
(a PDP-11
.BR a.out (5)
program, V7 magic 0407) as block 0 of the filesystem, before the superblock at
block 1.  This is the standard PDP-11 boot block: text plus data, at most one
block (512 bytes).  Without
.BR \-b ,
block 0 is left untouched.
.TP
.I blocks
Size of the filesystem in blocks.  If omitted, the size defaults to the image's
own size (from block 0, or from
.IR \-o ),
with a minimum of a small usable volume.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mkfs.filsys rp06-0.disk          # size the fs to the whole image
mkfs.filsys -o 18392 rp06-0.disk # a filesystem starting at block 18392
mkfs.filsys disk.dk 5000         # a 5000-block filesystem
mkfs.filsys -b /v7/mdec/rp06boot disk.dk   # install a boot block in block 0
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsck.filsys (1),
.BR findfs.filsys (1),
.BR mount.filsys (1),
.BR filsys (5),
.BR mkfs (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for the filsys project.
